WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
FAISS.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:06.067
Meta's library for fast vector search, living right inside your program.

3
00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:10.700
FAISS is an in-process approximate nearest neighbor library.

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.542
It's an object in your program's memory.

5
00:00:13.542 --> 00:00:15.767
No server, no network hop, no auth.

6
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:19.567
It also has no metadata filtering and no replication.

7
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:22.500
In exchange you get raw speed.

8
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.400
Give up one or two percent of recall, and you get back about two orders of magnitude.

9
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.067
FAISS ships every index type in the literature.

10
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:34.900
Flat indexes are exact, and make your benchmark ground truth.

11
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.533
HNSW is the graph index.

12
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:40.067
IVF partitions space into cells.

13
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:44.000
And product quantization compresses vectors to save memory.

14
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.667
Here's FAISS in practice.

15
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:48.800
One flat index for ground truth.

16
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:56.633
One HNSW index with the same inner product metric, thirty two links per node, and ef search as the recall knob.

17
00:00:56.933 --> 00:01:00.100
Everything runs in-process, and saving is one file.

18
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.500
So when do you use it?

19
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.533
FAISS fits experiments, benchmarks, and anything inside one machine's memory.

20
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:12.900
But the moment you need concurrent writers or filtered queries, you need a real database.

21
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.700
Learn FAISS first anyway.

22
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.000
In Mission Control RAG, every retrieval experiment runs on FAISS.

23
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:27.200
Chunking bake-offs, embedding comparisons, the flat against HNSW recall study, all in a notebook.

24
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.033
Rebuilding an index takes one line and zero infrastructure.

25
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:33.919
Production runs Qdrant, science runs FAISS.

26
00:01:33.919 --> 00:01:37.167
Knowing which is which is itself the interview answer.

27
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:39.201
Let's recap.

28
00:01:39.201 --> 00:01:42.467
FAISS is an in-process library, not a database.

29
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:45.300
It gives you every index type, and the knobs.

30
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.800
Use it for experiments and benchmarks.

31
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.814
Not for filtering or concurrent writers.

32
00:01:50.814 --> 00:01:51.900
Next up: Chroma.
